  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A complex and initially disorganized surface \nand upper level pattern today will begin to consolidate into a \nnotable East Coast coastal low mid to late-week, with impacts \nbeing felt from the Carolinas north.\n\nAloft, an upper level shortwave will lift north off the \nSoutheast U.S. coast, supporting the development of a weak area \nof low pressure along a stationary front. Meanwhile, a cold \nfront will continue to slowly sag south from Virginia into North\nCarolina over the next couple of days. The above-mentioned weak\nlow is then forecast to deepen along the consolidating, and \nstrengthening, frontal/baroclinic zone off the East Coast. \nDeepening of the low will become more rapid Thursday and Friday \nas an upper low translates from the Ohio Valley to the Mid-\nAtlantic Coast. The deepening will also be aided by the entrance\nregion of an upper jet which will reside over the Northern \nAtlantic.\n\nConfidence continue to increase regarding a sub-1000mb low \ndeveloping offshore during this time, and this is strongly \nsupported by deterministic, ensemble, and machine learning \nguidance. Because of the blocky nature of the upper level \npattern over the Atlantic, there is some uncertainty regarding \nthe eventual track of the low. While confidence is high \nregarding a notably deep low developing, confidence is lower \nregarding the magnitude of winds which especially factors in \nregarding coastal and marine impacts.\n\nThe most likely scenario at this point is for there to be an \ninitial round of showers and scattered thunderstorms late \ntonight into Tuesday as the above-mentioned cold front moves \ninto the area from the north. Rain and perhaps a few \nthunderstorms are then forecast to expand in coverage, and \nbecome more widespread, Tuesday night into Wednesday night as \nlift increases along the moistening frontal zone. During this \ntime, some guidance suggests an area of enhanced lift from the \nDelmarva region south into NE NC, and this is where the highest \nrainfall totals are expected. While instability will be waning \nwith time, rainfall totals of 2-3\" appear plausible in this \narea, which could support some minor hydro impacts in poor \ndrainage areas.\n\nThe strongest winds and greatest risk of coastal impacts is \nexpected Thursday into Friday, especially along the OBX. \nPersistent northeasterly winds coupled with a longer fetch \nacross the Atlantic should support long-period swell of 10-13s \nand wave heights of 8- 12ft in the surf zone for beaches from \nDuck to Cape Lookout. Not only will this create dangerous surf \nconditions, but this will also present a risk of beach erosion, \nocean overwash at vulnerable locations, and coastal flooding \n(both soundside and oceanside). As mentioned above, the strength\nand duration of the stronger winds varies some in the guidance \ndepending on the track of the low. Stay tuned as we continue to \nrefine the coastal impacts, and where coastal headlines may be \nrequired. Coastal impacts may last beyond Friday. In addition to\nthe coastal impacts, guidance supports a risk of reaching Wind \nAdvisory criteria for portions of the OBX Thursday into Friday.",
